WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.404
Graph neural networks are limited in what they can
compute, and the yardstick,

00:00:05.484 --> 00:00:07.449
the Weisfeiler-Lehman hierarchy,

00:00:07.529 --> 00:00:11.410
is hard to interpret and too coarse to separate
architectures.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:17.928
This ICML 2023 paper measures a graph network
instead by which equivariant polynomials it can compute.

00:00:18.008 --> 00:00:21.506
The authors give a complete basis for these polynomials,

00:00:21.586 --> 00:00:24.573
build tools to read off any model's exact power,

00:00:24.653 --> 00:00:29.940
and design a stronger network that reaches state of
the art on molecular benchmarks.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:36.596
Graph networks have bounded expressive power: only
some functions of a graph can be represented.

00:00:36.676 --> 00:00:42.025
The field measures this with the Weisfeiler-Lehman
hierarchy of isomorphism tests.

00:00:42.105 --> 00:00:46.593
But WL is combinatorial, giving no recipe to make a
network stronger,

00:00:46.673 --> 00:00:52.088
and its rungs are so far apart that very different
GNNs collapse to the same level.

00:00:52.168 --> 00:00:54.670
We need a finer, more actionable ruler.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:01:00.654
The authors argue the right way to grade a graph
network is by the quantities it can compute.

00:01:00.734 --> 00:01:02.121
Equivariant polynomials,

00:01:02.201 --> 00:01:06.094
polynomials of the adjacency matrix that respect
node relabeling,

00:01:06.174 --> 00:01:12.084
are natural candidates: subgraph counts and many
structural features are exactly such polynomials.

00:01:12.164 --> 00:01:15.690
Knowing their full space, and which a network can
evaluate,

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:20.030
gives both a fine-grained ruler and a to-do list of
missing operations.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:24.497
The core object is a basis for equivariant graph
polynomials.

00:01:24.577 --> 00:01:29.143
Each basis element is a directed multi-graph with a
marked red edge,

00:01:29.223 --> 00:01:31.739
and its value is a tensor contraction,

00:01:31.819 --> 00:01:36.043
an einsum over adjacency entries that sums over
internal nodes.

00:01:36.123 --> 00:01:40.825
Averaging monomials over all node permutations, the
Reynolds operator,

00:01:40.905 --> 00:01:42.396
makes them equivariant.

00:01:42.476 --> 00:01:43.831
To analyze a network,

00:01:43.911 --> 00:01:49.911
the authors treat it as a bank of primitive
contractions and give a linear-time algorithm

00:01:49.991 --> 00:01:52.848
deciding which polynomials it can assemble.

00:01:52.928 --> 00:01:58.314
Node-based banks equal one-WL, edge-based banks
equal two-FWL, that is three-WL.

00:01:58.394 --> 00:02:03.369
Building on this, PPGN plus plus feeds in
precomputed polynomial features,

00:02:03.449 --> 00:02:05.760
lifting it strictly above three-WL.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:11.303
The result is state of the art across the board.

00:02:11.383 --> 00:02:16.546
On ZINC-12K,

00:02:16.626 --> 00:02:21.789
PPGN plus plus with degree-six polynomial features
reaches 0.071 test error,

00:02:21.869 --> 00:02:27.031
beating CIN at 0.079 and GIN at 0.163.

00:02:27.111 --> 00:02:32.274
On ZINC-full it hits 0.020 and on Alchemy 0.109,
both best in class.

00:02:32.354 --> 00:02:37.517
And the features are model-agnostic: adding them to
a plain GatedGCN cuts its ZINC error from 0.265 to 0.106,

00:02:37.597 --> 00:02:42.760
all under a modest parameter budget.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:49.735
The lasting message: express a network's power as
which equivariant graph polynomials it can compute.

00:02:49.815 --> 00:02:53.833
Each is a small multi-graph evaluated by a tensor
contraction,

00:02:53.913 --> 00:02:58.921
a finer ruler than Weisfeiler-Lehman and a direct
list of missing operations.

00:02:59.001 --> 00:03:00.243
Acting on that list,

00:03:00.323 --> 00:03:06.257
precomputed polynomial features push ordinary graph
networks past three-WL to best-in-class

00:03:06.337 --> 00:03:08.240
molecular property prediction.
